<Base title="CPR / BLS Guide" activeSlug="cpr">
  <div class="breadcrumb"><a href={base}>MedKit</a> / References / <span>CPR / BLS Guide</span></div>
  <h1>CPR / BLS Guide</h1>
  <p class="cat-desc">Adult, child and infant CPR steps, compression:ventilation ratios and depths.</p>

  <div class="article" style="margin-top:18px">
    <div class="callout danger"><strong>Call for help first.</strong> If alone and untrained, call emergency services, then begin chest compressions immediately. This guide follows general BLS principles for reference — follow your local protocol and recent guidelines.</div>

    <h2>Adult CPR (≥ 8 yr / puberty)</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Check responsiveness and breathing (≤ 10 s).</li>
      <li>Activate EMS / get AED.</li>
      <li>Compressions: centre of chest, depth <strong>5–6 cm</strong>, rate <strong>100–120/min</strong>.</li>
      <li>Ratio <strong>30:2</strong> (untrained / lone rescuer). With advanced airway: continuous compressions.</li>
      <li>Give breaths over 1 s, chest rises; avoid excessive ventilation.</li>
      <li>Use AED as soon as available; follow prompts.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Child CPR (1 yr – puberty)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Depth: at least <strong>⅓ AP diameter (~5 cm)</strong>.</li>
      <li>Ratio: <strong>30:2</strong> single rescuer, <strong>15:2</strong> two rescuers.</li>
      <li>Prefer two-hand (or one-hand) compressions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Infant CPR (&lt; 1 yr)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Depth: at least <strong>⅓ AP diameter (~4 cm)</strong>.</li>
      <li>Compressions: two fingers (single) or thumbs-encircling hands (two rescuers).</li>
      <li>Ratio: <strong>30:2</strong> single, <strong>15:2</strong> two rescuers.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Compression quality checklist</h2>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Element</th><th>Target</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Rate</td><td>100–120 / min</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Depth (adult)</td><td>5–6 cm</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Recoil</td><td>Full, no leaning</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Interruptions</td><td>&lt; 10 s</td></tr>
    </table>

    <h3>FAQ</h3>
    <details class="faq"><summary>Hands-only CPR — when?</summary><div class="body">For witnessed adult cardiac arrest by a lay rescuer, uninterrupted chest compressions (100–120/min) without breaths is acceptable until an AED or help arrives.</div></details>
    <details class="faq"><summary>How do I know the depth is right?</summary><div class="body">Aim for about 5–6 cm in adults; use the "one-third chest depth" rule for children and infants. Feedback devices help.</div></details>
    <div class="callout">This is a study reference, not a certification course. Refresh with a hands-on BLS class regularly.</div>
  </div>
</Base>
